<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Moulin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Moulin was a French prefect and hero of the Resistance. Sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> into occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he united the main internal resistance movements and in May 1943 founded the National Council of the Resistance, which recognised de Gaulle's leadership. He was arrested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in June 1943 and died after torture. His ashes were moved to the Panthéon in 1964.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-moulin.jpg" alt="Jean Moulin" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Studio Harcourt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moulin_Harcourt_1937.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
